# Managed by home/srvs/nix-docs.nix; edits here are overwritten.
#
# ONE rule, on purpose.
#
# Everything else under docs/ is prose somebody wrote by hand — a runbook, a
# roadmap, an impact analysis — and for prose the right answer to "both machines
# edited this" is still a loud conflict that stops and asks a human. Union would
# silently duplicate paragraphs; last-writer-wins would silently delete one
# side's work. nix-docs.nix argues that at length and it has not changed.
#
# The boards are different in kind. Each is a STORE, not a document: an
# unattended writer (board-watch's agents), a GUI, and a five-minute sync window
# between the machines. Wedging the whole docs sync until someone notices —
# which is what an unresolved conflict does here — is the one outcome they must
# never produce.
#
# Since 2026-07-30 there is ONE BOARD PER HOST — `board.top.md` and
# `board.book.md`, each written only by its own machine and merely carried by
# the other as a backup and a history. [his, 2026-07-30] "i actually want to
# change it so neither board on top or air syncs ... i dont want that
# overwriting ... to overwrite anything i do on air. commits obviously will stay
# synced." So a genuine two-sided edit is now near impossible rather than
# routine, and this rule is the net for the case it happens anyway (a hand edit
# on the wrong machine, a restored backup).
#
# The driver tries the real 3-way merge FIRST, so non-overlapping edits both
# survive; only where the sides genuinely collide does it keep the more recent
# one whole. [his, 2026-07-29] "which version of the board should we use should
# be determined by which one was the most recent."
#
# Named one by one rather than globbed: `board*.md` would also swallow
# `agents/board-watch.md`, which is prose and must still conflict loudly. The
# pre-split `board.md` keeps its rule because git still merges history from
# before the rename.
#
# Requires merge.boardrecent.driver, which nix-docs-setup.sh registers on every
# run. Without that registration these lines are INERT and git silently falls
# back to the default merge — i.e. back to the wedged sync. Verified end-to-end
# by tools/board-merge-test.sh.
board.md merge=boardrecent
board.top.md merge=boardrecent
board.book.md merge=boardrecent
